:- module(bound_ambient_stability, [c/1]).
:- use_module(prelude).

:- function (g(string) -> string).
g(X) -> X.

:- function (s(string) -> string).
s(X) -> X.

:- function (h(A) -> A) requiring (g(A) -> A).
h(X) -> X.

% A rule head is an implementation site, so the head occurrence's T is
% a fresh rigid identity (a skolem), not a variable the body may bind.
% Two independent consequences, one per body goal:
%
%   * `h(1)` needs the callee's bound at int: g(int) -> int. The only
%     declared signature is g(string), and the ambient candidate the
%     head contributed is g(T#0) -> T#0 at the skolem, which is not
%     consistent with int either. No candidate survives, so the bound
%     is ruled out: YCHR-60012.
%   * `s(X)` passes the skolem to a string-typed parameter: an
%     ordinary inconsistency, YCHR-60001.
%
% Before rule heads became implementation sites, T was a flexible var
% shared with X's type, and this program tested something else — that
% an ambient candidate consistent only because its own variable was
% still unbound must not discharge a bound early. That hazard is what
% `sig_stable_witness` in `typechecker/solver.chr` guards; it is unreachable
% while every ambient signature is encoded at rigid tvars.
:- chr_constraint c(T) requiring (g(T) -> T).
c(X) <=> _Z is h(1), _W is s(X).
